Okay, so this morning we had a brief power blink here, so since I
had to reboot anyway, I figured I might as well take a few moments
and see if I could get NetHack4 to build under Windows.  (I have a
multiboot system; it spends almost all its time in Debian, but I
do have a working Windows install on the other partition.)

So I installed MinGW, on the theory that I'd probably need that,
and then went looking for either of the two build systems that
NH4 uses.

CMake is available for Win32 (Does anybody in the developed world
still use 32-bit systems?  Apparently someone still does.), but
not, as near as I can tell, for Windows x64.  Okay, no problem,
I thought, CMake is open source, so I'll just download the source
and build it...  Oh.  In order to build CMake, you need CMake.
That's just brilliant.

So I went looking for aimake, not knowning anything about it,
and discovered that in fact it's Perl-based.  Great!  That
should be easily portable to any OS/hardware combination that's
still in widespread use.  So I downloaded strawberry perl and
installed that, then attempted to follow the NH4 instructions:

C:\build\nitrohack-ais523\build>
   \strawberry\perl\bin\perl.exe .. \aimake

(The linebreak there is purely for usenet reasons.)

The next problem I ran into is this:
/usr/local/lib: No such file or directory at ..\aimake line 1142

Well, no, there wouldn't be.  I imagine this must mean the aimake
build system for NH4 is specifically for POSIX systems (and maybe
not all of those).

So my next thought was, with x64 being a superset of x86 for
backward compatibility reasons (which is why it succeeded where
Itanium went straight to Davey Jones' locker), perhaps I can use
the Win32 build of CMake to compile an x64 build of CMake, or
failing that maybe I could compile a working 32-bit NH4 with it.

Okay, so I downloaded the Win32 binary of CMake...

Yeah, that's not going to work.  The Win32 build of CMake appears
unable to read environment variables on 64-bit Windows:

C:\build\cmake-2.8.8\build>set
ALLUSERSPROFILE=C:\ProgramData
APPDATA=C:\Users\Jonadab\AppData\Roaming
CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=C:\MinGW\bin\gcc.exe
CMAKE_C_COMPILER=C:\MinGW\bin\gcc.exe
CommonProgramFiles=C:\Program Files (x86)\Common Files
[snip]
C:\build\cmake-2.8.8\build>
 "C:\Program Files (x86)\CMake 2.8\bin\cmake.exe" ..
[snip]
CMake Error:  your C compiler: "cl" was not found.  Please set
CMAKE_C_COMPILER to a valid compiler path or name.
CMake Error: your CXX compiler: "cl" was not found.  Please set
CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER to a valid compiler path or name.

Right, that's a dead end.  This is probably why the CMake people
haven't managed to produce a Windows x64 build yet.  (What I wonder
is, how did they get the first 32-bit Windows build working, when
it's not possible to compile on Windows without a working CMake?)

Maybe I can make something work under Cygwin?  So I downloaded that
and had another go with aimake...

Jonadab@warthog /cygdrive/c/build/nitrohack-ais523/build
$ perl ../aimake -i /opt/nethack4 ..
/strawberry/c/bin/../lib/gcc/x86_64-w64-mingw32/4.4.7/../../../;
/mingw/lib: No such file or directory at ../aimake line 1142

Yeah, I've got no idea what's going on there.  Maybe I can use
cygwin to build CMake?  The readme in the CMake source has some
instructions for that...

Jonadab@warthog /cygdrive/c/build/cmake-2.8.8
$ ./bootstrap

